% Percentage of undervote overall

\section{Putting School Board Elections in Context}

The authority given to school districts in Wisconsin to determine their own electoral 
rules and structure make Wisconsin a useful case in exploring the variation in 
school board electoral participation across contexts. In addition to the contextual 
variation across school districts, Wisconsin school districts also vary widely in 
size, wealth, performance, and student and household demographics. 

<<samplecomparisons, results='asis'>>=
rm(list=ls())
setwd("../../")
source("data/dataAssemble.R")
# load("data/cache/DataMergeVAP.rda")
source("data/cleanandprep_CANDIDACY.R")


SAMPSIZE <- length(unique(SBELEC$distid))
# Table showing sample and non-sample on key covariates
# Need to finish
## Per pupil expenditures
## 

mysub <- FULLDAT[FULLDAT$year == 2012, ]

selectVars <- c("TOTPOP", "member", "econ_disadv_per", 
                "millrate", "toprepshare", "PerBachelorOrAbove", "perwhite","avg_salary", "average_fringe", 
                "median_income", "OOH_share", "balance_member_lag", "teacherpupil_ratio", 
                "eqv_member")

rnames <- c("Total Population", "Students", 
            "% FRL", "Millrate", "Republican Share", "Share Bachelor +", "White %","Average Salary", 
            "Average Fringe", "Median Income", "Owner Occupied Share", 
            "Lagged Acct. Balance per Member", "Teacher-Pupil Ratio", "Property Value per Member")

a <- round(t(aggregate(mysub[, selectVars], 
                  by = list(sample = mysub$sample), mean, na.rm=TRUE)), digits=2)
b <- round(t(aggregate(mysub[, selectVars], 
                  by = list(sample = mysub$sample), sd, na.rm=TRUE)), digits=2)

a <- as.data.frame(a); names(a) <- c("Non-Sample (Mean)", "Sample (Mean)")
b <- as.data.frame(b); names(b) <- c("Non-Sample (SD)", "Sample (SD)")

ab <- as.data.frame(cbind(a[,2], b[,2], a[,1], b[,1]))
ab[3, ] <- ab[4, ] *100
ab[5, ] <- ab[6, ] *100
ab[7, ] <- ab[7, ] *100
ab[8, ] <- ab[8, ] *100
ab[12, ] <- ab[12, ] *100
ab[11, ] <- ab[11, ] * 10000
ab[13, ] <- ab[13, ] * 1000
names(ab) <- c("Sample (mean)", "Sample (SD)", "Out of Sample (mean)", "Out of Sample (SD)")
row.names(ab) <- c("Var", rnames)

print(xtable(ab[-1,], label="tab:samplecomp", caption = "Comparing school districts that provided board election results to those that did not and are not included in the study. Data 
             from the 2011-2012 school year." ), 
      format.args = list(big.mark=","), include.rownames=TRUE, size="scriptsize")

rm(a, b, ab)
@

% Describe above table

As shown in table \ref{tab:samplecomp}, there is little difference between districts 
in and out of the sample for the year 2012 on a range of measures of school district 
characteristics. Politically, the sample and the non-respondents reflect the rest of 
the state of Wisconsin -- evenly split. The groups have similar levels of student 
economic disadvantage as measured by eligibility for free and reduced price lunch and 
similar proportions of minority students in their schools. 
Their communities have similar levels of income, owner occupied housing, and levels 
of education. Teacher salary and fringe benefit rates are also nearly identical, as 
is the teacher-pupil ratio. 

The biggest discrepancy is that sample districts tend to be larger on average than 
out-of-sample districts in terms of student membership. This is no surprise as the largest 
Wisconsin school districts publish their election results online, making them among 
the easiest to obtain. The sample districts tend to have slightly smaller account balances or 
district savings accounts on a per pupil basis which is likely related to their larger size. 
Finally, sample districts tend to have less property value per member, or taxable 
assets to raise revenue from, owing to the slightly higher populations of sample 
districts and the inclusion of all urban areas. 

Similar tables are available for other years, but show the same general pattern. 
Thus, while the current sample does not include all of the school districts in the 
state of Wisconsin, it is safe to say that such school districts are representative 
of the diversity of Wisconsin public school districts at large. 

\subsection{School District Diversity}

Wisconsin, like other states with independent school districts, has a great diversity 
in school district size, demographic make up, and structure. To illustrate the wide 
array of organizations that school boards in Wisconsin govern, I present tables 
describing the five largest and five smallest school districts in Wisconsin and how 
they compare on a number of observable characteristics. These tables are intended 
to illustrate the range of organizational contexts that govern public K-12 education 
in Wisconsin and to illustrate the diversity of jurisdictions within which school 
board members are contesting elections. Table \ref{tab:small5} shows that the smallest 
school districts in Wisconsin have fewer than 100 students and fewer than 1,000 
residents. Contrast this with \ref{tab:big5} where the student population is 200 to 
300 times larger than the smallest districts. 

<<small5, results='asis'>>=
tmp.mat <- head(mysub[order(mysub$member), c("legal_name", selectVars)])
row.names(tmp.mat) <- tmp.mat[,1]
tmp.mat <- tmp.mat[,-1]
small5 <- as.data.frame(t(tmp.mat))
row.names(small5) <- rnames
small5[3, ] <- small5[3, ] * 100
small5[5, ] <- small5[5, ] * 100
small5[6, ] <- small5[6, ] * 100
small5[7, ] <- small5[7, ] * 100
small5[11, ] <- small5[11, ] *100
small5[10, ] <- small5[10, ] * 10000
small5[12, ] <- small5[12, ] * 1000
print(xtable(small5, label="tab:small5", caption = "Some attributes of Wisconsin's 5 smallest school districts. Data 
             from the 2011-2012 school year." ), 
      format.args = list(big.mark=","), include.rownames=TRUE, size="scriptsize")
rm(small5)
@

<<big5, results='asis'>>=
tmp.mat <- head(mysub[order(-mysub$member), c("legal_name", selectVars)])
row.names(tmp.mat) <- tmp.mat[,1]
tmp.mat <- tmp.mat[,-1]
big5 <- as.data.frame(t(tmp.mat))
row.names(big5) <- rnames
big5[3, ] <- big5[3, ] * 100
big5[5, ] <- big5[5, ] * 100
big5[6, ] <- big5[6, ] * 100
big5[7, ] <- big5[7, ] * 100
big5[11, ] <- big5[11, ] *100
big5[10, ] <- big5[10, ] * 10000
big5[12, ] <- big5[12, ] * 1000
print(xtable(big5, label="tab:big5", caption = "Some attributes of Wisconsin's 5 largest school districts. Data 
             from the 2011-2012 school year." ), 
      format.args = list(big.mark=","), include.rownames=TRUE, size="scriptsize")

@

% Bottom 5 districts

Contrast this to legislative districts, which are designed to be homogenous in 
terms of size. 

\clearpage

\section{Conclusion}

Wisconsin is a valuable case to study in the examination of school board elections. 
The statewide nature of this data collection allows for the exploration of the level 
of contestation and turnout in school board elections across a variety of contexts and 
within the same state political system. 

From the data collection we can already see that school boards are less frequently 
contested than many higher offices -- confirming prior work. School boards, despite 
their lower barrier to ballot entry and campaign costs, are contested less than half 
the time. School board elections are less prestigious, carry little financial benefit 
for members, and often require late evening meetings -- making them less attractive 
to potential candidates. School boards are also not shown to be a stepping stone 
to further political office, reducing their attractiveness to potential political 
candidates. 

Chapter 3 will seek to explore further the predictors of candidates 
emerging for office by exploring what local and state level factors may explain the 
emergence of a challenger to a school board election. Theory suggests that events 
like failed school bond referenda, turnover in district leadership, or fiscal pressure 
may drive local challenges. Theory also suggests that changing the powers of the school board, 
partisan identification of school board candidates, or political polarization may 
drive school board challenges to emerge statewide. Evidence of these state and 
local factors will be explored and evaluated to test whether theories of candidate 
emergence explain the case of school boards. 

Voters vote less in school board elections as well. In Wisconsin school board elections 
are held during the non-partisan spring elections, which have much lower turnout 
than partisan fall elections. In general, school board voter turnout hovers around 
20\% in school districts, but has plenty of variability. Voter turnout should be 
suppressed in school board elections because of both their non-partisan nature and 
their lower probability of being contested. School board researchers have long evaluated 
the dissatisfaction theory, which suggests that school boards exhibit higher contestation 
and voter turnout when local policy conditions change and the electorate is unsatisfied 
with the governance of the board. 

Chapter 4 will futher explore the puzzle of low school board turnout and identify 
if voter turnout does indeed respond to policy shocks. Local policy shocks such 
as leadership turnover and school fiscal challenges will be evaluated as well as 
statewide policy shocks like the passage of Act 10 and budget cuts. This will 
represent the fullest test of dissatisfaction theory to date and the first time 
exogenous policy shocks are leveraged to explore the causal relationship between 
changes in policy and voter turnout. 